{
  "version": "schemaxis.readiness/1",
  "routes": {
    "schemaxis.ffmpeg/1": {
      "guide": "docs/ffmpeg.md",
      "context": {
        "purpose": "Publish PNG frames, timed sprite sheets or an exclusive frame range from an existing silent MP4/H.264 or Matroska/FFV1 source. Output fps retimes the selected presentation frames. Optional deliverable requirements constrain dimensions, duration, bytes and per-frame RGB error. The muxReview operation also combines a captured silent Matroska/FFV1 video, a canonical PCM16 WAV and a strict SubRip file into one verified three-stream Matroska review movie (stream-copied, soft captions, machine-draft status declared). The extractAudio operation decodes the AAC-LC audio stream of one captured MP4/M4A recording (optionally with one H.264 stream) into a verified canonical PCM16 WAV and a machine-draft audio summary; AAC is lossy, so equality with the recording that was encoded is not claimed. The encodeAudio operation encodes one captured canonical PCM16 WAV to AAC-LC/M4A and publishes it only after a fresh native decode reproduces the exact source frame count on a zero-origin timeline, every interleaved sample is compared, and the exact rational mean absolute sample error and the byte size meet the authored budgets; AAC is lossy and the error budget is a waveform number, never a listening, recognition or identity certification.",
        "assumptions": "Exactly one node; explicit input order and constant frame rate. A matching ffprobe executable is required beside FFmpeg. The adaptor uses their public command-line interfaces. muxReview takes explicit local video, audio and caption files (or earlier verified artifacts); caption provenance is declared, never verified. extractAudio takes one explicit local .mp4 or .m4a file (or an earlier verified file artifact) and a required global streamIndex; nothing is inferred, and the captured file is an unauthenticated trust anchor. Running and verifying it need a trusted FFmpeg with ffprobe beside it (explicit executable, stored configuration or FFMPEG_EXECUTABLE; never PATH or receipt paths).",
        "constraints": "One node and no arbitrary filter graph; audio only through muxReview (canonical PCM16 WAV in) and extractAudio (AAC-LC in, PCM16 WAV out), and no operation resamples, mixes, trims or pads it. Existing-video sources: 1..600 frames, 32 MiB input, 192 MiB RGB, 4096 px per side, integer constant rate; no interlacing, non-square pixels or rotation/stream side data. MP4 edit lists use native presentation semantics. PNG alpha composites black; H.264 is lossy and needs even dimensions. muxReview: Matroska/FFV1 bgr0 video only; canonical mono/stereo PCM16 WAV up to 32 MiB; plain-text SubRip of at most 1000 cues and 256 KiB; audio within one video frame period of the video; captions inside the video; no burn-in, AAC, H.264, WebVTT, metadata, attachments or arbitrary streams. extractAudio: an intact, non-fragmented, self-contained .mp4/.m4a of at most 64 MiB with exactly one AAC-LC stream (mono or stereo, 8000 to 96000 Hz) chosen by streamIndex 0 to 15 and at most one H.264 stream; no extra audio, subtitle, data or cover-art streams, chapters, HE-AAC or other codecs; at most 600 s and a 32 MiB canonical WAV. The decoded frame count is listed by a bounded ffprobe pass (probe output of at most 4 MiB, so very large metadata tags are refused; at most 17600 packets and 17500 decoded frames, which covers 960-sample AAC-LC frames within 32 MiB but not a source with a very long edit-list lead-in); the first limit reached is refused as RESOURCE_LIMIT, never truncated. This is not exhaustive AAC-LC or MP4 coverage. encodeAudio: canonical PCM16 WAV only, mono or stereo at 16000, 22050, 44100 or 48000 Hz, up to 600 s and 32 MiB; explicit bitrateKbps 64/96/128/192, maxBytes and maxMeanPCMError; no resampling, mixing, normalising, trimming or padding; closed single-stream AAC-LC M4A with an empty metadata list whose sample-to-chunk and chunk-offset tables may describe several chunks (the muxer starts a new chunk before one reaches 1 MiB) and are accounted byte by byte against the mdat payload; verification decodes the published bytes again and does not re-encode, and requires the recorded ffprobe to be the sibling of the recorded ffmpeg (recorded paths are never executed).",
        "inspection": "Inspect video, its retained native decode/probe evidence and the fresh decode made when it is verified. Verify dimensions, frame count, rate and duration, and every decoded frame. FFV1 must match RGB bytes; H.264 mean RGB error is bounded at 16/255 per frame, and the stream must declare BT.601 limited-range colour (smpte170m/tv). These are bounded fidelity checks, not perceptual certification. Deliverable acceptance is independently reconstructed from authored requirements and the fresh native observations. For muxReview, inspect review.mkv with its retained probe, decoded video/audio and extracted captions: three-stream membership, decoded frames equal to the captured source, PCM bytes equal to the WAV data, and caption cues equal to the source in text and milliseconds. Verification re-derives these from the captured sources and decodes the published bytes again with a trusted FFmpeg. Recognition accuracy and human review are not certified. For extractAudio, inspect audio.wav and audio-summary.json with the retained probe, frame listing and native decode: every PCM byte of a fresh native decode of the captured MP4/M4A equals the published WAV and the decoded frame count equals the ffprobe audio-frame total. The WAV is a lossy machine-draft decode; equality with the original recording, recognition accuracy and human review are not certified.",
        "recovery": "INVALID_RESOURCE requires absolute PNG/metadata paths. Missing inputs need actual files, not placeholder paths. Correct dimensions or frame timing for native rejection; use FFV1 if H.264 exceeds the fidelity bound. Replan and rerun with retained failure logs. DELIVERABLE_NOT_MET retains a correct video with observed requirements and corrective next actions; revise sources, fps or codec before a fresh run. INVALID_FRAME_RANGE requires a nonempty zero-based inclusive-start/exclusive-end range within the complete source. UNSUPPORTED_VIDEO requires a supported silent progressive source with uniform presentation timestamps, or a file whose contents match its .mkv/.mp4 extension. For an existing video, INVALID_RESOURCE means the bytes are empty, too short for a container signature, or not Matroska/MP4 at all: supply an intact file. RESOURCE_LIMIT means the whole source exceeds 600 frames, 32 MiB or 192 MiB decoded: trim it before use. INVALID_RESOURCE with observed.diagnostics means FFmpeg reported errors on a recognised container (truncated or corrupt source): re-export it and run into a fresh directory. MEDIA_DURATION_MISMATCH: trim or pad the audio (SoX) or select the matching video range; nothing is trimmed or padded here. CAPTIONS_OUT_OF_RANGE: correct caption timing or use a longer video. UNSUPPORTED_AUDIO for muxReview audio: supply canonical PCM16 WAV (no LIST/fact chunks, float or 8-bit). UNSUPPORTED_AUDIO for extractAudio names the refused construct: supply an intact, non-fragmented, self-contained MP4/M4A with exactly one AAC-LC mono or stereo stream (not HE-AAC, another codec, extra audio, subtitle, data or cover-art streams, chapters, external data references or a start offset), re-muxed or transcoded first; a PCM WAV is not an extractAudio input. UNSUPPORTED_CAPTIONS: remove markup, control, bidirectional or invisible characters. INVALID_RESOURCE for audio or captions names the malformed construct and cue number. RESOURCE_NOT_FOUND names the missing input. Verifying encodeFrames, encodeSheet or encodeVideo needs a trusted FFmpeg with ffprobe beside it (INTERPRETER_NOT_FOUND otherwise; pass executable, configure FFmpeg or set FFMPEG_EXECUTABLE; running resolves the same choice and refuses with INTERPRETER_NOT_FOUND before creating its output directory when only PATH could supply FFmpeg). VERIFICATION_FAILED with observed.fresh true means the fresh decode of the retained publication contradicted the retained evidence; run into a fresh directory. extractAudio INVALID_ARGUMENT for streamIndex names the audio stream indices: use the global ffprobe index of the AAC stream (0 to 15). extractAudio RESOURCE_LIMIT means more than 64 MiB, 600 s or a 32 MiB WAV, ffprobe output over 4 MiB (remove or shorten large metadata tags) or a stream above the 17600-packet probe bound (shorten the source): a size or bounded-probe limit, not evidence that the source is invalid. extractAudio RUNNER_FAILED from a probe or decode stage (loader, allocation or unclassified output) means source validity was not established: inspect the retained stage logs and runtime, then retry in a fresh directory; INVALID_RESOURCE with observed.diagnostics means FFmpeg reported errors reading the recording. extractAudio resolves the trusted FFmpeg before it creates the output directory, for run and for verify (INTERPRETER_NOT_FOUND otherwise; pass executable, configure FFmpeg or set FFMPEG_EXECUTABLE); PATH is never used."
      },
      "examples": [
        {
          "id": "ordered-frames",
          "document": {
            "version": "schemaxis.ffmpeg/1",
            "nodes": [
